title: Paradigm 4.0 Decision — Context Engineering Framework Rebrand + Agent Maturation
summary: 'Decision to rebrand Paradigm as "The Context Engineering Framework" and bump to 4.0. Driven by industry cross-study analysis (30+ sources) proving Paradigm is ahead of the field, combined with the agent identity foundation shipped in 3.47.0. 4.0 encompasses: agent notebooks, model cascading, knowledge graduation, dynamic tool loading, agent permissions, context health scoring, specs as symbols, an agent registry (npm-for-agents), automated review pipeline, deep knowledge scan, and integrity checking. Two parallel workstreams: technical (this session) and content/branding (companion session).'
symbols_touched:
  - '#paradigm-mcp'
  - '#agent-loader'
  - '#agent-tools'
  - '#sentinel'
  - '#orchestration-tools'
files_created:
  - docs/specs/v4-technical.md
decisions:
  - id: v4-rebrand
    decision: Bump to Paradigm 4.0 with 'Context Engineering Framework' positioning
    rationale: Industry cross-study proves Paradigm is ahead on context engineering, multi-agent orchestration, and spec-first development. Agent identity layer provides the consumer-facing story. No one owns the 'context engineering framework' positioning yet.
  - id: v4-agent-registry
    decision: Build an agent registry (npm-for-agents) as a product at registry.useparadigm.dev
    rationale: 'Transferable agent profiles, notebooks, and patterns are publishable artifacts. Community sharing at concept level (not raw code) enables an ecosystem without privacy risk. Revenue: free public, paid private team registries.'
  - id: v4-parallel-tracks
    decision: 'Split work into two parallel tracks: technical (spec implementation) and content/branding (keynote, website, README, video series)'
    rationale: Creator needs to own the voice and narrative. Technical depth needs focused implementation. Both converge at 4.0 launch.
